(Regression: building a text-valued argument property into a buffer
 segfaulted — bug #52. LANGUAGE.md promises every sink shares one name
 resolver, so `{arguments's first}` renders the same whether printed,
 written, or built into a buffer; the buffer sink loaded its destination
 pointer into rdi before resolving the value, and resolving `arguments's
 first` puts the argument index in rdi to call `_get_arg`, so the append
 dereferenced an index instead of a buffer. All three buffer verbs —
 `set`, `copy` and `append` — went through that one sink, so all three
 crashed. The numeric properties survived by luck, and are kept here
 because they travelled the same broken path.)

a buffer called built is 128 bytes in size.

copy "first is {arguments's first}" to built.
Print built.

set built to "last is {arguments's last}".
Print built.

copy "arguments:" to built.
append " {arguments's first}" to built.
append " and {arguments's last}" to built.
Print built.

copy "count is {arguments's count}, first is {arguments's first}" to built.
Print built.

(A list built into a buffer renders as its pointer, which is unstable
 from run to run — and Print renders it exactly the same way, so the
 shared-resolver promise holds and the rendering question belongs to
 bug #44's family. All this case has to prove is that it does not crash.)
copy "{arguments's all}" to built.
Print "all arguments survived the copy".
